To ensure that VR applications match their purpose, i.e., deliver an experience that delivers a good user experience and enables the fulfilment of the goals for which it was designed, it is of utmost importance to evaluate them properly. To this end, the body of knowledge is consensual that the sense of presence is a key factor in evaluating a VR application’s effectiveness [4], [5], [6], which can be further complemented with other variables such as usability, satisfaction, cognitive load, or cybersickness, depending on the purpose of the VR application being evaluated. However, despite the common understanding that presence can be defined as the sense of ‘‘being there’’ (i. e., in the VE), different definitions were proposed for this phenomenon and how it develops.

